nora: Quick FYI — JS quickstart snippet is dangerously written for server use. It shows sending the API key in the query string and uses fetch without checking response.ok, so people copy it into backend code and leak creds in logs. Noticed during a partner POC.
devex-lead: oh no. which file?
nora: docs/quickstart/js/README.md. snippet is basically:
```js
const url = `https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate?key=${API_KEY}`;
const res = await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
const data = await res.json();
console.log('response', data);
```
sam: yep that's bad — query param tokens get logged and show up in monitoring. also no retry/backoff for 429 and no content-type header.
nora: proposed fix: send auth in Authorization header, check res.ok, parse Retry-After, and redact logs. small snippet:```js
const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY}`,
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(payload),
});
if (!res.ok) {
  if (res.status === 429) {
    const retryAfter = res.headers.get('retry-after');
    const waitMs = retryAfter ? Number(retryAfter) * 1000 : 1000;
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, waitMs));
    // retry once in sample
  }
  throw new Error(`request failed: ${res.status}`);
}
const data = await res.json();
console.log('response: <redacted>');
```
devex-lead: good. add a one-liner recommending use of node-fetch with AbortController or axios and mention exponential backoff+jitter for production. also call out not to log tokens and to use env vars/secrets.
nora: will open a PR to replace the snippet, add a short note in onboarding quickstart (JS section) about not putting keys in URLs, and add a tiny test in docs CI that lints the samples for basic security patterns (search for 'key=' in quickstarts).
sam: can we also add a brief parity note pointing to similar advice in python/rust/csharp quickstarts, so customers get consistent guidance?
